## Abstract / key result
{% if tierA %}This analysis identified **{{ tierA|length }} Tier A** candidate variant(s) consistent with the clinical presentation of Hyper-IgE syndrome (HIES). The leading candidate is a {{ tierA[0].zygosity }} variant in **{{ tierA[0].gene }}** ({{ tierA[0].consequence }}), segregating under a {{ tierA[0].model|replace('_',' ') }} model.{% elif tierB %}No Tier A variant was identified; {{ tierB|length }} Tier B variant(s) of uncertain significance are reported.{% else %}No HIES-panel variant met Tier A/B reporting criteria under the applied models and thresholds.{% endif %}

## Methods

**Samples & pedigree.** Whole-exome sequencing (Twist Human Core Exome 2.0 + RefSeq, paired-end 151 bp, Illumina) was performed for {{ family|selectattr('sequenced')|list|length }} members of one family:
{% for m in family %}{{ m.iid }} ({{ m.role }}, {{ m.sex }}{{ ', not sequenced' if not m.sequenced else '' }}); {% endfor %}
{% if mz_groups %}The affected twins ({{ mz_groups|map('join',' + ')|join('; ') }}) are monozygotic and were treated as genetic replicates (a candidate is required to be genotype-concordant across the pair; discordant genotypes were flagged as technical artifacts).{% endif %}

**Primary pipeline (CallForge).** FASTQ reads were aligned to GRCh38 (no-alt) with bwa-mem2, duplicate-marked, and base-recalibrated (GATK BQSR, dbSNP b157 known-sites). Per-sample QC gating (mean target depth, on-target fraction, duplication, sex) preceded joint genotyping (GATK HaplotypeCaller → GenomicsDB → GenotypeGVCFs) and hard-filtering. Copy-number was called with CNVkit. Variants were annotated with Ensembl VEP (cache v112; consequence, MANE/canonical transcript, HGVS, SIFT, PolyPhen, gnomAD exome/genome allele frequencies) and vcfanno (ClinVar {{ meta.provenance.get('clinvar_date','2026-06') if meta.provenance else '2026-06' }}, PhyloP 100-way).
{% if meta.provenance %}Tool/DB versions: see provenance (`{{ meta.provenance.get('git_commit','n/a') }}`).{% endif %}

**Interpretation (familyforge).** The multi-sample annotated VCF was normalised (biallelic split) and restricted to a curated HIES/high-IgE inborn-error-of-immunity panel of {{ meta.n_panel_genes }} genes. Pedigree-aware Mendelian models were applied — de novo/dominant, autosomal-recessive homozygous, compound heterozygous (maternal/paternal phase inferred; father not sequenced), and X-linked recessive — with per-genotype quality gates (GQ ≥ {{ '{:.0f}'.format(0) }}, see config) and MZ-twin concordance. Candidates were tiered by an ACMG-style rubric combining ClinVar, population frequency (dominant ≤ {{ meta.af_dominant_max }}, recessive ≤ {{ meta.af_recessive_max }}), predicted consequence, in-silico damage, conservation, gene disease-mechanism, and segregation.

